From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 20:19:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: let agents manage chat link rules and tell them the
 active syntaxes

Agents set up most of a user's tooling, so they should be able to add a
link rule when asked ("link BST-123 to our Jira"), and they should write
references in the form the chat links instead of wrapping them in code.

- sys_link_rules lists, adds, updates and removes rules. The runner proxies
  it to /v1/link-rules: it applies one change to the rules it read, checks
  the list with the server's validator and saves it with that version, so a
  concurrent edit from Settings is refused rather than overwritten. It is a
  framework tool group (tool_groups.link_rules) and rides the native relay.
- Rules run in every viewer's browser, so every add, update and remove asks
  the user first: __ask_on_link_rule_change joins the approval policies
  every engine carries, and the tool skips the no-policy fast path, like the
  scheduled-task tools. Listing is not gated.
- A framework instruction names each enabled rule's syntax (#123 and
  owner/repo#123, session:<id>, ABC-123 for a short form) so agents write
  them as plain text. SDK harnesses get it per turn from rules the runner
  rereads after a minute; native Claude and Codex get it at launch, from the
  runner or from omnigent claude/codex, so a rule changed mid-session reaches
  their prompt on the next launch. A failed read just leaves it out.

Not upstream yet: proposed from feat/chat-link-rules; drop this copy once it merges.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
